Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. JUDGE MILLER'S LIBRARY - NIGHT

A fire roars. BUCK, a powerful 140-pound mix of St. Bernard and Scotch shepherd, lies at the JUDGE's feet. The room is warm, paneled, lined with books.

NARRATOR
Old longings nomadic leap, chafing at custom's chain; again from its brumal sleep wakens the ferine strain.

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

BUCK rules the sun-kissed Santa Clara Valley. He swims in the cement tank with the JUDGE's sons, escorts MOLLIE and ALICE on twilight rambles, and guards the grandsons down to the fountain in the stable yard. Fox terriers yap from windows. Toots the pug and Ysabel the hairless stay inside.

BUCK stalks among the terriers like a king.

EXT. ORCHARD - NIGHT

MANUEL, a gardener's helper, leads BUCK on a rope through the trees. BUCK trots willingly.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

A lone STRANGER waits. Money chinks between MANUEL and the STRANGER.

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

MANUEL doubles a stout rope around BUCK's neck under the collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

The STRANGER grunts. BUCK accepts the rope with quiet dignity until the ends pass to the STRANGER. BUCK growls. The rope tightens. BUCK lunges. The STRANGER grapples him, twists, and throws him. The rope shuts off BUCK's breath. His eyes glaze.

INT. BAGGAGE CAR - NIGHT

BUCK wakes. The STRANGER's hand is near. BUCK's jaws close on it. The STRANGER chokes him out again.

INT. SHED BEHIND SALOON - NIGHT

The STRANGER's hand is wrapped in a bloody handkerchief. The SALOON KEEPER counts.

STRANGER
All I get is fifty for it. An' I wouldn't do it over for a thousand, cold cash.

SALOON KEEPER
How much did the other mug get?

STRANGER
A hundred.

SALOON KEEPER
That makes a hundred and fifty. He's worth it.

They file the brass collar from BUCK's neck and fling him into a cagelike crate.

INT. CRATE IN SHED - NIGHT

BUCK lies nursing his wrath. The SALOON KEEPER peers in by tallow candle. BUCK's joyful bark twists into a growl.

EXT. SEATTLE BACK YARD - DAY

Four men carry the crate into a high-walled yard. RED SWEATER, stout in a sagging red sweater, signs for it. BUCK hurls himself against the bars. RED SWEATER brings a hatchet and club.

RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

BUCK launches. The club drops him. Again and again. BUCK charges until a final blow crumples him senseless.

RED SWEATER
He's no slouch at dog-breakin', that's wot I say.

BUCK wakes. RED SWEATER offers water and raw meat. BUCK drinks and eats.

RED SWEATER
Be a good dog and all'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you.

BUCK endures the pat on his head. He has learned the club.

EXT. SEATTLE YARD - DAY

Other dogs arrive in crates. RED SWEATER breaks them. Money passes. Strangers take dogs away. BUCK watches, glad when he is not chosen.

PERRAULT, a weazened French-Canadian, arrives.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! How moch?

RED SWEATER
Three hundred, and a present at that.

PERRAULT grins and pays. CURLEY, a good-natured Newfoundland, and BUCK are led away.

EXT. DECK OF THE NARWHAL - DAY

BUCK and CURLEY watch Seattle recede. PERRAULT takes them below to FRANCOIS, a swarthy half-breed.

INT. 'TWEEN-DECKS OF THE NARWHAL - DAY

BUCK and CURLEY join SPITZ, a big white dog, and DAVE, a gloomy wheeler. SPITZ steals BUCK's food. FRANCOIS's whip sings and strikes SPITZ first. BUCK recovers the bone.

EXT. DECK OF THE NARWHAL - DAY

The propeller stops. Snow falls. BUCK sniffs it, licks it, yelps at the burn. Onlookers laugh.

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

The camp is confusion and peril. CURLEY approaches a husky. The husky strikes, leaps away, rips CURLEY's face. Thirty huskies close in. CURLEY is buried screaming beneath the mass. FRANCOIS swings an axe. Three men with clubs scatter them. CURLEY lies limp and torn. SPITZ laughs with his tongue. BUCK hates him.

FRANCOIS fastens a harness on BUCK. BUCK hauls firewood. DAVE nips his hindquarters. SPITZ leads and growls.

PERRAULT returns with BILLEE and JOE. SPITZ thrashes BILLEE. JOE faces him, snarling.

PERRAULT secures SOL-LEKS, the one-eyed Angry One. SOL-LEKS slashes BUCK's shoulder for approaching his blind side. BUCK learns to avoid it.

EXT. CAMP AT HEAD OF LAKE BENNETT - NIGHT

BUCK tries the tent. PERRAULT and FRANCOIS drive him out with curses and pans. He wanders, cold. He returns, finds the team gone, circles, then sinks into BILLEE's snow hole. Warmth rises. BUCK digs his own and sleeps.

EXT. TRAIL ABOVE SHEEP CAMP - DAY

Nine dogs in harness swing up the canyon. BUCK runs between DAVE and SOL-LEKS. They correct him with teeth. FRANCOIS's whip cracks less. PERRAULT examines BUCK's feet.

BUCK steals bacon when PERRAULT's back is turned. DUB is blamed and beaten.

NARRATOR
It marked the decay of his moral nature, a vain thing and a handicap in the ruthless struggle for existence. Under the law of club and fang, whoso took such things into account was a fool.

BUCK's muscles harden. He scents the wind a night ahead. On still cold nights he points his nose at a star and howls wolflike, the cadences of his ancestors surging through him.

EXT. CAMP ON SHORE OF LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

BUCK finishes his fish and returns to his nest under the rock. SPITZ occupies it. A warning snarl rises.

BUCK springs. The two dogs tumble from the nest in a tangle.

FRANCOIS
A-a-ah! Gif it to heem, by Gar! Gif it to heem, the dirty t'eef!

SPITZ circles, snarling. BUCK circles. An oath from PERRAULT, the crack of a club, and a yelp shatter the night. Starving huskies pour from the darkness.

The team bursts from their nests. Three huskies rip BUCK's head and shoulders. DAVE and SOL-LEKS fight side by side. JOE crunches a foreleg. PIKE finishes the crippled dog. BUCK seizes a throat; blood sprays his muzzle. SPITZ lunges from the side and tears BUCK's throat.

PERRAULT and FRANCOIS drive the horde back with clubs. The team flees across the ice. SPITZ charges BUCK from behind. BUCK braces, then runs.

EXT. FOREST NEAR LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

The nine dogs limp into the trees. BILLEE whimpers, ear shredded. JOE has lost an eye. DUB drags a hind leg. DOLLY's throat hangs open. At dawn they return to camp. Half the grub is gone. PERRAULT's moccasins and FRANCOIS's whip lashings have been eaten.

FRANCOIS
Ah, my frien's. Mebbe it mek you mad dog, dose many bites. Mebbe all mad dog, sacredam! Wot you t'ink, eh, Perrault?

PERRAULT shakes his head. They harness the stiff team and push on.

EXT. THIRTY MILE RIVER - DAY

Wild water runs between ice bridges. PERRAULT breaks through, saved by his pole. He builds fire after fire, clothes steaming. The sled plunges; DAVE and BUCK are dragged half-frozen from the current. They circle a fire until ice melts from their coats.

SPITZ breaks through and drags the team to the edge. BUCK strains backward, forepaws on the rim. DAVE strains behind him. FRANCOIS hauls on the traces until his tendons crack.

Later the rim ice collapses on both sides. PERRAULT scales the cliff. The dogs are hoisted one by one on a rope of lashings. Night finds them back on the river.

EXT. TRAIL TO DAWSON - DAY

BUCK limps. His feet have softened through generations of ease. At camp FRANCOIS rubs them half an hour and fashions moccasins from his own tops. BUCK lies on his back, paws in the air, refusing to move without them. PERRAULT grins.

EXT. TRAIL NEAR PELLY - DAY

DOLLY howls, a long heartbroken wolf cry. She springs at BUCK. He flees in panic, DOLLY one leap behind. They race across islands and back channels. FRANCOIS calls from a quarter mile away. BUCK doubles back. FRANCOIS swings the axe; it crashes into DOLLY's skull.

BUCK staggers against the sled, sobbing for breath. SPITZ drives him down and tears flesh to bone. FRANCOIS's lash falls again and again on SPITZ.

PERRAULT
One devil, dat Spitz. Some dam day heem keel dat Buck.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck two devils. All de tam I watch dat Buck I know for sure. Lissen: some dam fine day heem get mad lak hell an' den heem chew dat Spitz all up an' spit heem out on de snow. Sure. I know.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - NIGHT

A snowshoe rabbit bolts from under DUB. The team gives chase. Fifty police huskies join. BUCK leads the sixty-dog pack, whining, body flashing over moonlit snow. The rabbit flees light as frost.

SPITZ cuts across a neck of land and leaps from the bank. His teeth break the rabbit's back in midair. The pack howls. BUCK drives into SPITZ shoulder to shoulder. They roll. SPITZ slashes and leaps clear.

They circle, snarling. The sixty huskies form a silent ring, eyes gleaming, breaths rising. BUCK rushes. Fang clashes fang. SPITZ slashes and gives ground. BUCK feints the shoulder charge, drops low, and crunches SPITZ's left foreleg. He repeats the blow on the right.

SPITZ staggers on three legs. The circle tightens. BUCK springs. Shoulder meets shoulder. SPITZ vanishes under the dark ring. BUCK stands alone on the moon-flooded snow, the dominant primordial beast who has made his kill and found it good.

EXT. CAMP ON THE YUKON - DAY

Francois examines Buck's wounds by the fire. Perrault surveys the gashes.

FRANCOIS
Eh? Wot I say? I spik true w'en I say dat Buck two devils.

PERRAULT
Dat Spitz fight lak hell.

FRANCOIS
An' dat Buck fight lak two hells. An' now we make good time. No more Spitz, no more trouble, sure.

EXT. CAMP ON THE YUKON - DAY

Perrault packs the sled. Francois harnesses the team. Buck trots to the lead position. Francois brings Sol-leks forward instead. Buck springs on Sol-leks and drives him back.

FRANCOIS
Eh? eh? Look at dat Buck. Heem keel dat Spitz, heem t'ink to take de job.

Francois slaps his thighs. He grabs Buck by the scruff and drags him aside. Buck circles, snarling. Francois returns with a club. Buck retreats just beyond reach, eyes on the club.

EXT. CAMP ON THE YUKON - DAY

Francois and Perrault chase Buck around the camp for an hour. They hurl clubs. Buck dodges every throw. He keeps his distance but never runs away.

FRANCOIS
Now, by Gar, I feex you!

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops the club. Buck trots in, laughing, and takes the lead position. His traces are fastened. The men run alongside as the sled breaks out onto the river trail.

EXT. THIRTY MILE RIVER - DAY

Buck leads at a bound. The team leaps as one. They cover in one day what once took ten.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE TO WHITE HORSE RAPIDS - DAY

Buck sets the pace. The sled flies. One man tows behind on a rope.

EXT. WHITE PASS - DAY

The team tops the pass and drops toward the sea. Lights of Skaguay appear below.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

Francois and Perrault throw out their chests. Mushers and dog-busters crowd the team. Francois calls Buck, throws his arms around him, and weeps. He and Perrault walk away.

EXT. TRAIL TO DAWSON - DAY

The Scotch half-breed takes charge. A dozen dog-teams move the mail. Buck maintains discipline. The dogs pull heavy loads through new snow.

EXT. CAMP NEAR CASSIAR BAR - DAY

Dave lies in his nest by the fire, too weak to rise. The half-breed removes him from the traces. Dave attacks Sol-leks, then flounders alongside in the soft snow. He bites through Sol-leks's traces and stands in his old place.

HALF-BREED
He has earned it.

The half-breed harnesses Dave again. Dave pulls, crying out from pain, then falls and is dragged.

EXT. CAMP NEAR CASSIAR BAR - DAY

Dave lies gasping in the snow, eyes on the departing team. A revolver-shot cracks behind the river timber. The sleds move on. Buck and the team know what has happened.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

The Salt Water Mail team arrives. Buck and his mates are worn to one hundred fifteen pounds. Their feet drag. Pike limps in earnest. Sol-leks limps. Dub favors a wrenched shoulder.

HALF-BREED
Mush on, poor sore feets. Dis is de las'. Den we get one long res'.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

Three days later the team stands spent before a Government agent. HAL, nineteen, belt bristling with cartridges, and CHARLES, middle-aged with a fiercely twisted mustache, count out coins. MERCEDES watches, hands fluttering.

HAL
We'll take the lot. Harness and all.

The half-breed turns away without a word. Buck watches the mail drivers pass out of his life.

EXT. NEW OWNERS' CAMP - DAY

The tent sags half-stretched. Unwashed dishes lie about. Mercedes chatters advice while Charles and Hal roll the canvas into a bundle three times too large. Three neighboring men grin and wink.

ONLOOKER
You've got a right smart load. I wouldn't tote that tent.

MERCEDES
Undreamed of! However could I manage without a tent?

The men load the sled. It lists, top-heavy. Hal seizes the gee-pole.

HAL
Mush!

The dogs strain but cannot move the sled. Hal raises the whip. Mercedes seizes it.

MERCEDES
You mustn't! Promise you won't be harsh.

HAL
They're lazy. You've got to whip them.

An onlooker steps forward.

ONLOOKER
Runners are froze fast. Throw your weight on the gee-pole.

Hal rocks the sled free. The team lunges under the lash. The overloaded sled tips on the first turn, scattering gear. The dogs bolt up the street, dragging the capsized sled and Hal.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

Kind citizens catch the team and gather the load.

ONLOOKER
Half the load and twice the dogs if you expect to reach Dawson.

Charles and Hal pitch tent again and cull the outfit. Mercedes weeps over each discarded article, then attacks her men's belongings like a tornado. That evening they buy six Outside dogs.

EXT. TRAIL ABOVE SKAGUAY - DAY

Fourteen dogs start north. Buck leads without heart. The Outside dogs flounder. Days pass. The party makes poor time, breaks camp late, stops often to rearrange the load. Hal doubles rations, then Mercedes steals extra fish. Food runs low. Hal cuts the ration.

EXT. TRAIL - DAY

Dub falls and cannot rise. Hal shoots him with the Colt. The Newfoundland dies next, then the pointers. Only Buck, Sol-leks, Joe, Pike, and Teek remain. The team staggers, perambulating skeletons. Mercedes rides the sled until the dogs collapse. Charles and Hal drag her off; she sits in the trail until they unload and carry her back.

EXT. FIVE FINGERS - DAY

Hal trades the revolver for frozen horse-hide. The dogs gnaw the iron-like strips. Billee falls. Hal strikes him with the axe and cuts the carcass from the traces. Koona follows the next day.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

The five survivors reach the mouth of White River. The dogs drop as though struck dead. JOHN THORNTON whittles an axe-handle. Hal uncoils the whip.

HAL
They told us the bottom was dropping out. We'll go on to Dawson.

THORNTON
Only fools could have made it. I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice.

Hal's whip falls. Sol-leks rises, then Teek, then Joe. Pike struggles up. Buck lies still. The lash cuts him again and again. Thornton rises, eyes wet.

THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

Hal draws his hunting-knife. Thornton knocks it away with the axe-handle, then cuts Buck's traces. Hal gathers Mercedes and Charles. The sled pulls out onto the river ice.

Buck lifts his head. The sled's rear drops. A section of ice gives way. Dogs and humans vanish into the black water. Thornton kneels beside Buck, searching for broken bones.

THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

Buck lies by the river bank. SKEET, a small Irish setter, washes his wounds. NIG, a huge black dog, half bloodhound and half deerhound, lies nearby. JOHN THORNTON whittles. Buck's muscles swell. He romps with Skeet and Nig. Thornton joins the games.

THORNTON
God! you can all but speak!

Buck seizes Thornton's hand in his teeth. Thornton laughs.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

Buck lies at Thornton's feet, watching every expression. Thornton turns and meets the gaze. Buck follows Thornton from the tent and back. At night Buck creeps to the flap and listens to Thornton's breathing.

A deep call sounds from the forest. Buck rises, steps toward the trees, then turns back to the fire.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

HANS and PETE arrive on the raft. Buck ignores them until they stand near Thornton, then tolerates them.

EXT. CLIFF ABOVE TANANA - DAY

Thornton, Hans, and Pete sit on the crest. Buck stands at Thornton's shoulder. Thornton sweeps his arm over the three-hundred-foot drop.

THORNTON
Jump, Buck!

Buck leaps. Thornton grapples him at the edge. Hans and Pete drag them back.

PETE
It's uncanny.

THORNTON
No, it is splendid, and it is terrible, too. Do you know, it sometimes makes me afraid.

PETE
I'm not hankering to be the man that lays hands on you while he's around.

HANS
Py Jingo! Not mineself either.

EXT. CIRCLE CITY SALOON - NIGHT

BLACK BURTON strikes Thornton at the bar. Buck rises from the corner with a roar, seizes Burton's throat, and is dragged off by the crowd. A miners' meeting clears him.

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK RAPIDS - DAY

Hans and Pete snub the poling-boat with rope. Thornton poles from the boat. Buck paces the bank. The boat flips. Thornton is flung into the current. Buck dives, reaches him, and is ordered to shore.

Thornton clings to a rock. Hans and Pete attach the rope to Buck and launch him. Buck is jerked under, hauled out, then launched again. He strikes Thornton like a battering ram. They are hauled to the bank. Thornton finds three broken ribs on Buck.

THORNTON
That settles it. We camp right here.

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON - NIGHT

MATTHEWSON slams a sack of gold dust on the bar.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't.

JIM O'BRIEN
Sure. Though it's little faith I'm having, John, that the beast can do the trick.

EXT. STREET - DAWSON - DAY

Matthewson's sled stands loaded with twenty fifty-pound sacks of flour, runners frozen. Thornton kneels beside Buck.

THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck whines. Thornton steps back.

THORNTON
Now, Buck.

Buck tightens the traces.

THORNTON
Gee!

Buck swings right. The runners crack free.

THORNTON
Haw!

Buck swings left. The sled pivots.

THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck lunges. The sled jerks forward, then moves steadily. Thornton runs behind. At the hundred-yard mark the crowd roars. Thornton falls to his knees beside Buck, shaking him.

THORNTON
You can go to hell, sir.

Buck seizes Thornton's hand in his teeth. The onlookers draw back.

EXT. STEWART RIVER TRAIL - DAY

Thornton, Pete, and Hans lead the sled team up the narrowing river. Buck trots at the head, pulling steadily. The men hunt game along the way. They pass the Mayo and McQuestion and push into the peaks.

EXT. LOST CABIN VALLEY - DAY

The party sets up a spruce-bough lodge beside a broad placer. Gold shows yellow in the pans. They work every day, sacking dust in moose-hide bags. Buck lies by the fire, watching the forest.

THORNTON
Never was there such a dog.

PETE
When he was made, the mould was broke.

HANS
Py jingo! I t'ink so mineself.

Buck rises and slips into the trees.

EXT. FOREST - NIGHT

Buck stalks through moonlight. A long howl rises ahead. He enters a clearing and faces a lean timber wolf. They circle, then touch noses. The wolf lopes away. Buck follows.

EXT. DIVIDE - DAY

Buck cuts a great bull moose from its herd. He harries the animal for four days, never letting it rest or drink. At twilight on the fourth day the bull falls. Buck feeds and rests beside the kill.

EXT. CAMP CLEARING - DAY

Buck returns at a lope. He finds Nig dead with an arrow through him. Farther on a sled dog thrashes in its death struggle. Hans lies face down, bristling with arrows. The Yeehats dance among the ruins of the lodge. Buck charges.

Buck rips the chief's throat, then tears into the others. Arrows fly. The Yeehats panic and flee. Buck drags several down among the trees.

EXT. POOL EDGE - DAY

Buck follows Thornton's trail to the water. Skeet lies at the edge. No trace leads out of the pool. Buck broods beside the muddy water.

EXT. FOREST CLEARING - NIGHT

A full moon lights the valley. The wolf pack pours into the clearing. Buck stands motionless. The boldest wolf leaps. Buck breaks its neck. More attack. He pivots and slashes until the pack draws back. The wild brother advances, whining. They touch noses. An old wolf howls. Buck joins the chorus.

The pack runs into the woods. Buck runs with them, side by side with the wild brother, singing the song of the pack.

Buck, a great wolf with a splash of white on his chest, leads the pack through pale moonlight. He pauses once above the yellow stream that flows from rotted moose-hide sacks, howls once, long and mournful, then vanishes among the trees.

FADE OUT

THE END
